Lise","key":"kjærannelisepau","last":"Kjær","locid":"f29b4cea-cbd5-49cb-8efd-52cfc50a2e4a","name":"Kjær, Anne Lise","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Lin","key":"adrianlinpau","last":"Adrian","locid":"22f09c63-9682-48bf-ac30-86511f913409","name":"Adrian, Lin","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Louise Victoria","key":"johansenlouisevictoriapau","last":"Johansen","locid":"23daa69d-5ff2-4caa-9d49-22ff107bbdda","name":"Johansen, Louise Victoria","orcid":"0000-0003-3482-9274","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Katrine Meldgaard","key":"kjærkatrinemeldgaardpau","last":"Kjær","locid":"de12158d-82f2-461a-af08-b9227d1cefef","name":"Kjær, Katrine Meldgaard","orcid":"0000-0001-7994-5186","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"issn":["01051121"],"issue":"5","journal":"Retfærd: Nordisk Juridisk Tidsskrift","pages":"63-85","publisher":"Djøf Forlag","status":"p","title":"Retfærd: Nordisk Juridisk Tidsskrift","volume":"37"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-c126c9fd-cebb-4530-bbfb-4197332a7fc6","sdu-f39aba58-c779-4f5c-af48-8155e0b287d2"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Imagining Law : A Pilot Study of Entering Law Students' Discourse about Law, Legal Education and Legal Professions","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-100.00-045913","year":"2014","yearsub":"2014"}
{"abstract":"Strange Culinary Encounters: Stranger Fetishism in Cooking Shows In this paper, we will examine the ways in which the encountering of 'other' food cultures is played out in the two travelogue cooking shows Gordon's Great Escape and Jamie's Italian Escape, arguing that despite their ‘noble’ intentions and ‘enlightened’ cosmopolitan approach to meeting the other (culinary culture), ultimately, Jamie and Gordon's respective culinary adventures work to create a social hierarchy in their own favor. Inspired by Sara Ahmed’s work on stranger fetishism, we will investigate how the two protagonist chefs Jamie Oliver and Gordon Ramsay imagine, meet and evaluate the ‘other’ food cultures in these programs, paying special attention to how the encounter with the local Indian and Italian is imagined to be a gateway to an authentic and/or primitive experience. To unpack the programs and the experiences within them, we use critical discourse analysis and the readings strategies of cultural studies and postcolonial theory and a focus on how classic, colonial power structures are reproduced in contemporary, globalized food culture. We will consider what potential this methodological framework holds for unpacking cultural encounters, and show how we have used it to deconstruct how ‘authentic’ food is done and met in Gordon's Great Escape and Jamie's Italian Escape. In doing so, we will argue that this way of reading cultural encounters ultimately opens for an alternative understanding of the central concepts of the ‘strange’, the ‘encounter’ and ideas of authenticity and, importantly, how the three intersect.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Leer, Jonatan","Kjær, Katrine Meldgaard"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-154420"],"event":{"end":"2014-11-14","place":"Roskilde, dk","start":"2014-11-13","title":"METHODOLOGIES WITHIN THE FIELD OF CULTURAL ENCOUNTERS","year":"2014"},"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"l